{"context":"sto:compliance-telemetry (M5) requires, per repository and period: sessions observed; sessions that read a constraint before their first write; gate failures; violations repaired within the same session. bug:compliance-telemetry-premise-unsupported records that none of the four is derivable from stored data today: there is no session identifier anywhere, no retrieval or gate-refusal log, and no session/audit table; what capture persists is provenance, sourcePromptHash, confidence, sourceCommitSha and captureMeta only. The design is further bound by an accepted finding: research-LLM-context-efficiency.md rejects server-side session state ('No server session state in EKA'; 'Reject = EKA maintaining per-agent session table'). A sessionId can therefore only be a client-supplied payload value, and telemetry can only be immutable append-only records. The alternative paths (a new CKO type, per-session CKO lines) collide with immutability, with the publish/note gates, and with the read-only read path.","decision":"Telemetry is an append-only JSONL event log, one file per session at docs/telemetry/\u003csessionId\u003e.jsonl, carried as a source-only RSF attachment (ADR-027) with no store-schema, corpus-token or envelope change. A single new command surface `eka telemetry` both emits and queries: `emit --event \u003ckind\u003e --session \u003cid\u003e [--subject] [--constraint] [--gate] [--reason]` appends one record; `list --session \u003cid\u003e|--repo --since --until [--json]` reads. sessionId is opaque and client-supplied (the host agent exports EKA_SESSION, or mints a ULID once via a stateless `eka telemetry session`); EKA never mints-and-stores an id and never infers a session from timestamp adjacency. The write and gate paths instrument opt-in when a sessionId is present (publish success -\u003e first-write/write; publish conformance block, transition refusal, verify fail -\u003e gate-refusal; verify pass -\u003e gate-pass); the read path stays read-only and is emitted explicitly by the caller. Each record carries prevHash = SHA-256 of the previous record's bytes, so the append-only chain is tamper-evident without any server state. When no sessionId is supplied the record is written with an empty sessionId and sessionSource 'unattributed' - never a fabricated id. 'telemetry' joins captureTransportDirs so telemetry file names never drive artifact classification. M5 becomes a pure consumer of this stream and invents no field. Per the review decision: the home is docs/telemetry/, 'repaired in session' is measured conservatively as a gate-refusal on a subject followed by a gate-pass on the same subject in the same session (labelled an upper bound), and no standard release is taken - a spec CKO documents the convention.","consequences":"Positive: M5's four numbers become derivable (two exactly, two as honestly-labelled conservative derivations); no corpus, envelope, store-schema or standard change is needed; the data survives a clone through the existing source-only transport; the append-only chain plus the explicit no-fabrication rule make a session boundary decidable from an explicit id only. Negative: telemetry lives under docs/ (mixing operations data with the knowledge tree) and is shared by clone, so it must never carry prompt content, only hashes and references; read coverage is opt-in, so 'read before first write' is an undercount that must be stated, not a false zero. Risk: the conservative 'repaired in session' definition (refusal then later pass) is an upper bound, not proof of a causal repair - the report must label it as such, or an explicit gate-repair event must be added later.","alternativesConsidered":"(a) A new CKO type `tel:`: rejected - `tel-` is outside the closed token set, so it is a corpus change requiring standard 1.5; every event would need the artifact sections, a dimension and the publish/note gates (the telemetry that records gate failures would itself be gated - chicken and egg); the read path would become a publish path and stop being read-only; and thousands of event lines would pollute the knowledge graph, which ADR-035 explicitly guards against. (a2) A `ses:` line per session with one instanceVersion per event: rejected as the telemetry home - the token is legal so it needs no 1.5, but reads cannot be published, every event would pass Validate and the gates, the model collides with the existing `ses:` singleton execution-state projection (ADR-037), and the per-event overhead is absurd. (b) A `sessionId` envelope field on every CKO: rejected as the mechanism - it changes the unit.json shape (standard 1.5), pollutes every unit, and captures only writes (not reads or gate failures). (b2) A single growing attachment rewritten via Upsert: rejected - Upsert is mutation, not append-only. (c) A new store table for events: rejected - that is exactly the server-side session state the research rejects.","appliesTo":["cmd/telemetry.go"]}